{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42472 - PROTEIN:4968",
  "verbalization": "N-Methyltyramine is implicated in the target pathway leading to ZR-75-1 through multiple indirect routes, all involving the Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9. This enzyme is a common target of several compounds, including 2,4-Bis[(4-hydroxyphenyl)methyl]-5-methoxy-3-[2-(3-methoxyphenyl)ethyl]phenol, Michaolide J, and 5-(2-Thienylethynyl)-2-thiophenemethanol. These compounds, in turn, are linked to ZR-75-1, suggesting a potential mechanism where N-Methyltyramine may influence the activity of these enzymes, which then interact with ZR-75-1. The evidence supports a possible role for N-Methyltyramine in modulating the function of SDR family members, which could ultimately affect ZR-75-1. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential dependency on SDR family members, which may be a key mechanism in the interaction between N-Methyltyramine and ZR-75-1.",
